--!strict
--!optimize 2
--!lint ConstLocal

const .{
    elements.{ Heading, Paragraph, UnorderedList, ListItem, Link },
    components.{ Code, CodeBlock },
    site.{ Page, PageLink, TableOfContents },
} = require("@chimera")
const luwu = require("./sample")
const .{ sample, terminal } = require("./terminal")
const performance = require("@self/performance")
const .{ h1, h2, h3 } = Heading

const health = sample("health")

return Page("Classes and Traits", {
    h1("Classes and Traits"),
    Paragraph({
        "A class represents a concept from your program's domain. It groups that concept's data (its fields) with ",
        "its behavior (its methods) and encapsulates the data to protect the class's invariants, the rules that ",
        "must always hold.",
    }),
    Paragraph("Each instance of a class, called an object, holds its own values for those fields."),
    Paragraph("A trait is behavior that many classes share, written once and implemented by each of them."),
    Paragraph({
        "Classes and objects are primitives in Luwu, like numbers and strings. ",
        Code("type()"), " of a class is ", Code("\"class\""), ". ",
        Code("type()"), " of an object is ", Code("\"object\""), ".",
    }),
    TableOfContents(),

    h2("From metatables to classes"),
    Paragraph("In Luau, objects are usually tables with a metatable. Each one needs some setup code:"),
    CodeBlock([====[
        const Account = {}
        Account.__index = Account

        function Account.new(owner: string)
            return setmetatable({ owner = owner, balance = 0 }, Account)
        end

        export type Account = typeof(Account.new(...))

        function Account.deposit(self: Account, amount: number)
            self.balance += amount
        end

        const account = Account.new("Ada")
        account:deposit(50)
        print(account.balance)
    ]====], "luau"),
    Paragraph({
        "That's well-typed Luau. The type comes from ", Code("typeof(Account.new(...))"), ". ",
        "Each method takes ", Code("self: Account"), " by hand.",
    }),
    Paragraph("In Luwu, the same thing is a class:"),
    luwu([====[
        class Account(owner: string)
            balance = 0

            function deposit(self, amount: number)
                self.balance += amount
            end
        end

        const account = Account("Ada")
        account:deposit(50)
        print(account.balance)
    ]====]),

    h3("Type checking"),
    Paragraph({
        "Metatables are hard for the type checker to follow, and types and autocomplete often break on them. ",
        "With a class, the checker knows every field and method of every object, and a misspelled field is a ",
        "type error instead of a silent nil.",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        "Classes and traits are nominally typed: what matters is the name, not the shape. ",
        "Two classes with the same fields are still different types, ",
        "and one can't be passed where the other is expected. ",
        "A trait's name is a type too, and an object of any class that implements the trait fits it.",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        "Metatables still work, and Luwu doesn't ask you to give them up. ",
        "Luwu still types tables with metatables structurally like Luau but checks them better through ",
        "improvements like type references and method calls on union types.",
    }),

    h3("Performance"),
    Paragraph({
        "Classes are also faster than metatable objects. ",
        "Objects take less memory than tables, and reading or writing a field skips the key lookup a table needs. ",
        "At ", Code("--!optimize 2"), ", Luwu can also copy small methods straight into the code that calls them. ",
        "You don't need to do anything special to get this: write classes, and the compiler does the rest.",
    }),
    Paragraph({ PageLink(performance, "See Performance Innovations →") }),

    h3("Inheritance through traits"),
    Paragraph({
        "Upstream Luau is adding classical inheritance through ", Code("open class"), " and ", Code("extends"), ". ",
        "Luwu keeps the useful parts of inheritance and calls them traits. ",
        "A trait gives classes shared methods with default implementations they can override, ",
        "lists members each class is expected to provide, and can build on other traits.",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        "What Luwu leaves out is the confusing part of classical inheritance. ",
        "Building an object only ever runs its own class's constructor. ",
        "There's no is-a identity either: an object belongs to exactly one class, and ",
        Code("class.isinstance"), " checks that class alone. ",
        "Whether an object has some trait's behavior is a separate question, answered by ",
        Code("class.implements"), ". Traits are covered further down.",
    }),

    h2("Classes"),

    h3("Your first class"),
    Paragraph("The parameters after the class name are its primary constructor."),
    Paragraph("Each parameter becomes a field of the object."),
    Paragraph({ "Functions that take ", Code("self"), " are methods. You call them with ", Code(":"), "." }),
    luwu([====[
        class Dog(name: string, age: number)
            function bark(self)
                print(`{self.name} barked!`)
            end
            function birthday(self)
                self.age += 1
            end
        end

        const oreo = Dog("Oreo", 3)
        oreo:bark()
        oreo:birthday()
        print(oreo.age)
    ]====]),
    Paragraph("Calling a class like a function builds a new object."),
    Paragraph("Passing the wrong number or type of arguments is a type error."),

    h3("Plain old data (POD) classes"),
    Paragraph({
        "Some classes just hold data, like a named table or a struct. Luwu calls these plain old data (POD) classes. ",
        "Give each field a default value, and leave out the constructor.",
    }),
    Paragraph("Then build objects from a table with the POD constructor. Fields you leave out keep their defaults."),
    luwu([====[
        class Settings
            volume = 0.8
            fullscreen = false
            player_name = "Player"
        end

        const defaults = Settings()
        const custom = Settings { fullscreen = true, player_name = "Ada" }
        print(defaults.volume, custom.fullscreen, custom.player_name)
    ]====]),
    Paragraph({
        "Default values are created fresh for every object. ",
        "Two objects never share a default table by accident.",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        class Inventory
            items: { string } = {}
        end

        const a = Inventory()
        const b = Inventory()
        table.insert(a.items, "sword")
        print(#a.items, #b.items)
    ]====]),

    h3("Static functions"),
    Paragraph({ "A function without ", Code("self"), " belongs to the class itself. Call it with a dot." }),
    Paragraph("Static functions are a good place for alternative ways to build an object."),
    luwu([====[
        class Temperature(celsius: number)
            function from_fahrenheit(fahrenheit: number): Temperature
                return Temperature((fahrenheit - 32) * 5 / 9)
            end
            function describe(self): string
                return `{self.celsius}°C`
            end
        end

        print(Temperature.from_fahrenheit(212):describe())
    ]====]),

    h3("Computed fields"),
    Paragraph({
        "A field's default can use the constructor's parameters. ",
        "That's how you clean up or derive data on the way in.",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        class Username(raw: string)
            text = string.lower((string.gsub(raw, "%s+", "_")))
        end

        const name = Username("Ada Lovelace")
        print(name.text, name.raw)
    ]====]),
    Paragraph({ "The parameter stays a field too: ", Code("name.raw"), " is still there." }),

    h3("Constructors with __init"),
    Paragraph({
        "Add an ", Code("__init"), " function when building an object needs custom logic. ",
        "Luwu creates the object, then calls ", Code("__init"), " with it and your arguments. Reach for it when:",
    }),
    UnorderedList({
        ListItem("the constructor takes parameters that shouldn't become fields"),
        ListItem("fields are computed from the arguments over several steps"),
        ListItem("construction calls other functions, or validates its input"),
        ListItem("the constructor needs to yield, like waiting on a file or a network request"),
    }),
    Paragraph("This point is built from text like \"3,4\". It keeps x and y, but not the text:"),
    luwu([====[
        class Point
            x: number
            y: number

            function __init(self, text: string)
                const x, y = string.match(text, "^(%-?%d+),(%-?%d+)$")
                self.x = tonumber(x) or 0
                self.y = tonumber(y) or 0
            end
        end

        const point = Point("3,4")
        print(point.x, point.y)
    ]====]),
    Paragraph({ "A class has either a primary constructor or ", Code("__init"), ", not both." }),

    h3("Public, private and const"),
    Paragraph({ "Mark a member ", Code("private"), " to keep it inside the class." }),
    Paragraph("Code outside the class can't read or change it. Trying is a type error, and an error at runtime."),
    Paragraph({ "Mark a field ", Code("const"), " when it should never change after the object is built." }),
    Paragraph("Once one member has public or private, every member needs one."),
    luwu([====[
        class BankAccount(
            public const owner: string,
            private balance: number = 0
        )
            public function deposit(self, amount: number)
                if amount > 0 then
                    self.balance += amount
                end
            end
            public function get_balance(self): number
                return self.balance
            end
        end

        const account = BankAccount("Ada")
        account:deposit(50)
        account:deposit(-10)
        print(account.owner, account:get_balance())
    ]====]),
    Paragraph("Only the class's own methods can change the balance. Every deposit goes through the same check."),

    h3("Protecting invariants"),
    Paragraph({
        "Some data is only valid when certain properties always hold: health stays between 0 and its maximum, ",
        "an age is never negative, a position is never NaN. Those properties are the class's invariants, and ",
        "encapsulation is how a class protects them. When the fields are private and every change goes through ",
        "the class's own methods, no code outside it can leave the data in an invalid state.",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        "This ", Code("Health"), " class represents a player's health points. Its constructor and ", Code("current"),
        " are private, every change goes through ", Code("clamped"), ", and its metamethods let you use ",
        Code("+"), ", ", Code("-"), ", ", Code("=="), " and ", Code("<"), " on it directly. Printing it draws a ",
        "healthbar:",
    }),
    CodeBlock(health.source):copyable(),
    Paragraph({
        "The healthbar's colors come from seal's ", Code("@std/io/colors", "plaintext"), " library. Save the ",
        "example as ", Code("health.luwu", "plaintext"), " and run it with ",
        Link("seal", "https://github.com/seal-runtime/seal/pull/295"):new_tab(), ":",
    }),
    CodeBlock("seal ./health.luwu", "bash"):copyable(),
    terminal(health.output),
    Paragraph({
        "Overhealing by 500 stops at 100/100 because every result passes through ", Code("math.clamp"),
        ". From outside the class, ", Code("hp.current = -20"),
        " is a type error and a runtime error, and ", Code("Health(-5, 100)"), " fails because the constructor ",
        "is private.",
    }),

    h3("Operators on objects"),
    Paragraph({
        "Metamethods let your objects work with operators like ", Code("+"), " and ", Code("=="),
        ". Name a method after the metamethod.",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        Code("Money"), " keeps whole units and cents in separate fields because a floating point ",
        Code("number"), " can't hold most fractions exactly (", Code("0.1 + 0.2"), " is ",
        Code("0.30000000000000004"), "). Its constructor and fields are private, and every amount comes from ",
        Code("Money.usd"), ", ", Code("Money.eur"), " or ", Code("Money.gbp"), ". Those reject negative amounts ",
        "and fractions of a cent before an object ever exists.",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        type Currency = "USD" | "EUR" | "GBP"

        class Money private (
            private const units: number,
            private const cents: number,
            public const currency: Currency
        )
            public function usd(units: number, cents: number): Money
                return Money.of(units, cents, "USD")
            end
            public function eur(units: number, cents: number): Money
                return Money.of(units, cents, "EUR")
            end
            public function gbp(units: number, cents: number): Money
                return Money.of(units, cents, "GBP")
            end
            private function of(units: number, cents: number, currency: Currency): Money
                if
                    units % 1 ~= 0
                    or cents % 1 ~= 0
                    or units < 0
                    or cents < 0
                    or cents > 99
                then
                    error("expected whole units and 0 to 99 cents", 3)
                end
                return Money(units, cents, currency)
            end
            private function check_currency(self, other: Money)
                if self.currency ~= other.currency then
                    error(`can't mix {self.currency} and {other.currency}`, 3)
                end
            end
            public function __add(self, other: Money): Money
                self:check_currency(other)
                const cents = self.cents + other.cents
                const units = self.units + other.units + cents // 100
                return Money(units, cents % 100, self.currency)
            end
            public function __eq(self, other: Money): boolean
                return self.units == other.units
                    and self.cents == other.cents
                    and self.currency == other.currency
            end
            public function __lt(self, other: Money): boolean
                self:check_currency(other)
                if self.units ~= other.units then
                    return self.units < other.units
                end
                return self.cents < other.cents
            end
            public function __tostring(self): string
                return string.format("%d.%02d %s", self.units, self.cents, self.currency)
            end
        end

        const total = Money.usd(19, 99) + Money.usd(5, 1)
        print(`{total}`)
        print(total == Money.usd(25, 0), total < Money.usd(30, 0))
    ]====]),

    h3("Checking what an object is"),
    Paragraph({ Code("class.isinstance"), " tells you whether a value is an object of a class." }),
    Paragraph("Inside the check, Luwu knows the value's type."),
    luwu([====[
        class Circle(radius: number) end
        class Square(side: number) end

        const function area(shape: Circle | Square): number
            if class.isinstance(shape, Circle) then
                return math.pi * shape.radius ^ 2
            end
            return shape.side ^ 2
        end

        print(area(Circle(1)), area(Square(3)))
        print(class.name(Square(3)))
    ]====]),

    h2("Traits"),
    Paragraph("Traits are how classes inherit behavior in Luwu."),
    Paragraph("A trait lists what it expects from a class, and what it gives the class in return."),
    Paragraph("A class can implement as many traits as it needs."),

    h3("Sharing behavior"),
    Paragraph({
        "Here, ", Code("Lootable"), " gives any class a working ", Code("drop"), " method. ",
        "It only asks the class for a ", Code("loot"), " list.",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        trait Lootable
            expect loot: { string }

            function drop(self): { string }
                const dropped = self.loot
                self.loot = {}
                return dropped
            end
        end

        class Chest(loot: { string }) implements Lootable end
        class Slime(hp: number, loot: { string }) implements Lootable end

        const chest = Chest({ "gold", "map" })
        const slime = Slime(5, { "goo" })
        print(#chest:drop(), #chest:drop(), slime:drop()[1])
    ]====]),
    Paragraph("Both classes get the trait's methods without writing them again."),

    h3("Expected members"),
    Paragraph({ Code("expect"), " lists what an implementing class must provide: fields, functions or both." }),
    Paragraph("Leaving one out is a type error, and an error when the class is created."),
    luwu([====[
        trait Describable
            expect const name: string
            expect function details(self): string

            function describe(self): string
                return `{self.name}: {self:details()}`
            end
        end

        class Sword implements Describable
            const name = "Iron sword"
            damage = 7

            function details(self): string
                return `deals {self.damage} damage`
            end
        end

        print(Sword():describe())
    ]====]),

    h3("Overriding a trait's function"),
    Paragraph("A class can replace a trait's function with its own version."),
    Paragraph({
        "The class can still reach the trait's version with ", Code("Trait.function(self)"), ".",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        trait Greeting
            function greet(self): string
                return "Hello!"
            end
        end

        class Pirate implements Greeting
            function greet(self): string
                return Greeting.greet(self) .. " Arr!"
            end
        end

        print(Pirate():greet())
    ]====]),
    Paragraph("The override must take the same arguments the trait's version does. Extra arguments must be optional."),

    h3("Building on other traits"),
    Paragraph({ "A trait can ", Code("needs"), " another trait. Implementing it implements both." }),
    luwu([====[
        trait Named
            expect const name: string
        end

        trait Speaker needs Named
            function introduce(self): string
                return `I'm {self.name}.`
            end
        end

        class Robot implements Speaker
            const name = "R2"
        end

        print(Robot():introduce())
    ]====]),

    h3("Final members"),
    Paragraph({ "Mark a trait's member ", Code("final"), " when classes must not replace it." }),
    Paragraph("A final function without self is a static function on the trait, for helpers that belong with it."),
    luwu([====[
        trait Shape
            expect function area(self): number

            final function total_area(shapes: { Shape }): number
                local total = 0
                for _, shape in shapes do
                    total += shape:area()
                end
                return total
            end
        end

        class Rectangle(width: number, height: number) implements Shape
            function area(self): number
                return self.width * self.height
            end
        end

        class Circle(radius: number) implements Shape
            function area(self): number
                return math.pi * self.radius ^ 2
            end
        end

        print(Shape.total_area({ Rectangle(2, 3), Circle(1) }))
    ]====]),

    h3("Traits with parameters"),
    Paragraph({
        "A trait can take parameters, like a class's constructor. ",
        "The class passes them when it implements the trait.",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        trait Stackable(max_stack: number = 64)
            count = 1

            function add(self, amount: number): number
                const room = self.max_stack - self.count
                const added = math.min(room, amount)
                self.count += added
                return amount - added
            end
        end

        class Arrow implements Stackable end
        class Potion implements Stackable(8) end

        print(Arrow():add(100), Potion():add(100))
    ]====]),

    h3("Working with any implementer"),
    Paragraph({
        "A trait is also a type. ", Code("{ Shape }"), " is a list of anything that implements ", Code("Shape"), ".",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        Code("Trait"), " is any object whose class implements ", Code("Trait"), ", ", Code("class<Trait>"),
        " is the type of the class value of any class that implements ", Code("Trait"), ", and ",
        Code("trait<Trait>"), " is the type of the trait value itself. This might be confusing, so here's an example:",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        trait Animal -- Animal is trait<Animal>
            expect species: string
        end

        class Cat( -- Cat is class<Animal>
            name: string,
            age: number
        ) implements Animal
            species = "Felis catus"
        end

        const nyla = Cat("Nyla", 14) -- nyla is Animal
    ]====]),
    Paragraph({
        Code("class.isinstance"), " asks whether a value is an object of one exact class, while ",
        Code("class.implements"), " asks whether the value's class implements a trait. Inside either check Luwu ",
        "narrows the value's type. Once ", Code("describe"), " knows it has an ", Code("Animal"), ", reading ",
        Code("thing.species"), " type checks:",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        trait Animal
            expect species: string
        end

        class Cat(name: string) implements Animal
            species = "Felis catus"
        end

        class Dog(name: string) implements Animal
            species = "Canis familiaris"
        end

        class Rock() end

        const function describe(thing: unknown): string
            if class.implements(thing, Animal) then
                return `an animal of species {thing.species}`
            elseif class.isinstance(thing, Rock) then
                return "a rock"
            end
            return "something else"
        end

        print(describe(Cat("Nyla")))
        print(describe(Dog("Rex")))
        print(describe(Rock()))
    ]====]),
    Paragraph({
        "Written with only ", Code("class.isinstance"), ", ", Code("describe"), " would need a check for ",
        Code("Cat"), ", another for ", Code("Dog"), " and one more for every animal class anyone adds later, ",
        "even in code it has never seen. A single ", Code("class.implements"), " check covers them all. ",
        Code("class.isinstance"), " only takes classes, and when it's given a trait luwu-analyze reports ",
        Code("Expected this to be a class, got trait 'Animal'", "plaintext"), ".",
    }),

    h3("Traits as factories"),
    Paragraph({
        "Sometimes code has to build an object without knowing its class. chimera, the library this site is built ",
        "with, parses HTML back into objects: it reads each component's name from attributes like ",
        Code("data-element=\"Card\"", "html"), ", and apps register components chimera has never heard of. A ",
        "trait can build those objects. Give the trait a ", Code("__create"), " function and calling the trait ",
        "like a class runs it to pick a concrete class and return a new object of that class.",
    }),
    Paragraph({
        "This ", Code("Component"), " trait keeps a registry of ", Code("class<Component>"), " values by class ",
        "name. Each component class is registered once with ", Code("Component.register"), ", and ",
        Code("Component(\"Button\")"), " then builds a ", Code("Button"), ":",
    }),
    luwu([====[
        const components: { [string]: class<Component>? } = {}

        trait Component
            expect function __init(self)
            expect function render(self): string
            function __create(name: string): Component
                const cls = components[name] or error(`no component called {name}`, 2)
                return cls()
            end
            final function register(cls: class<Component>)
                components[class.name(cls)] = cls
            end
        end

        class Button() implements Component
            function render(self): string
                return "<button>Save</button>"
            end
        end

        class Spinner() implements Component
            function render(self): string
                return `<div class="spinner"></div>`
            end
        end

        Component.register(Button)
        Component.register(Spinner)

        -- names a parser found in the page's data-element attributes
        for _, name in { "Spinner", "Button" } do
            print(Component(name):render())
        end
    ]====]),
    Paragraph({
        "Only traits that define ", Code("__create"), " can be called. Calling the ", Code("Animal"),
        " trait from earlier raises ", Code("trait 'Animal' can't be called: it has no '__create'", "plaintext"), ".",
    }),

    h2("Things to know"),
    UnorderedList({
        ListItem("Classes and traits are defined at the top level of a file."),
        ListItem("Objects have exactly the fields their class lists. Reading a missing field is an error."),
        ListItem({ "Objects compare by identity unless the class defines ", Code("__eq"), "." }),
        ListItem("A class's name is also a type: an object of that class."),
    }),
}, "classes-and-traits.html")
    :add_page(performance)
